{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mass.QDiffProlongationBaseOperator","lean:module:QOU.Mass.ColorCurveQIntegerForm"],"declarations":11,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.qshift_bracket_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.qshift_bracket_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":186,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.lean#L186-L194","doc":"**`q → 1`: the q-shift difference degenerates to the classical derivative.** At\n`q = 1` the whole difference is zero (the shift is trivial), and the *quotient* — the\nbracket on the right — becomes `∑ (k+1)z^k` rungwise by `gradedZInt_one`. Stated as the\nbracket identity so that no division by `q − 1` is needed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.qshift_leading_coeff_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.qshift_leading_coeff_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.lean#L179-L184","doc":"**The `X³` coefficient is untouched**, which is why the degree drops: it is the\nconstant `[1]_z = 1`, carrying no `z`, so the q-shift annihilates it. The classical\nderivative does the same thing for the same reason. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.qshift_colorSpectralCubic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.qshift_colorSpectralCubic","kind":"theorem","line":156,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.lean#L156-L177","doc":"**The q-shift of the colour spectral cubic, in closed form.**\n\n`C_{q·z}(X) − C_z(X) = (q−1)·z·( g₁·X² + g₂·X + g₃ )` with `gᵢ = gradedZInt q z i`.\n\nDividing by `(q−1)z`, the Jackson q-derivative of the colour cubic is\n\n  `D_q C(X) = gradedZInt q z 1 · X² + gradedZInt q z 2 · X + gradedZInt q z 3`\n\nagainst `C_z(X) = [1]_z·X³ + [2]_z·X² + [3]_z·X + [4]_z`: **the same shape, ladder index\ndropped by one, `[k]_z` replaced by `gradedZInt q z (k−1)`.** The operator preserves the\nq-integer-curve class.\n\nBranch-free by construction: the coefficients are symmetric functions of the sheets,\nso `a_k(q·z)` involves no choice of sheet — which is where obstruction (ii) of the W1\nbean lives, and why it is absent here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.gradedZInt_three_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.gradedZInt_three_eq","kind":"theorem","line":152,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.lean#L152-L154","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.gradedZInt_two_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.gradedZInt_two_eq","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.lean#L149-L150","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.gradedZInt_one_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.gradedZInt_one_eq","kind":"theorem","line":144,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.lean#L144-L147","doc":"The first three rungs, written out. `D_q [2]_z = 1`, `D_q [3]_z = 1 + [2]_q·z`,\n`D_q [4]_z = 1 + [2]_q·z + [3]_q·z²` — the three coefficients of the colour cubic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.gradedZInt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.gradedZInt_one","kind":"theorem","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.lean#L134-L142","doc":"**`q → 1` degeneration.** `gradedZInt 1 z n = ∑_{k<n} (k+1)·z^k`, the classical\n`d/dz [n+1]_z`. So the q-graded ladder limits to the classical one, exactly as the base\noperator's `[n]_1 = n` does. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.jackson_zInt_numerator","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.jackson_zInt_numerator","kind":"theorem","line":115,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.lean#L115-L132","doc":"**The Jackson q-derivative of a z-integer, cleared.**\n\n`[n+1]_{q·z} − [n+1]_z = (q−1)·z·∑_{k<n} [k+1]_q·z^k`.\n\nDividing by the Jackson denominator `(q−1)z` gives `D_q [n+1]_z = gradedZInt q z n`. The\ninduction step is exactly the base-operator file's monomial identity\n`jackson_monomial_numerator_commRing`, applied one degree at a time — so this is the\ncomposite of the two existing W1 bricks and nothing more. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.gradedZInt_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.gradedZInt_succ","kind":"theorem","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.lean#L110-L113","doc":"The ladder step: `gradedZInt` grows by one term of `[n+1]_q·z^n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.gradedZInt_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.gradedZInt_zero","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.lean#L107-L108","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.gradedZInt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.gradedZInt","kind":"def","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QShiftColorCoefficientLadder.lean#L97-L105","doc":"The **q-graded z-integer** `∑_{k<n} [k+1]_q · z^k`.\n\nIt is the Jackson q-derivative of `[n+1]_z` (`jackson_zInt_numerator`), and it is the\nq-deformation of the classical `d/dz [n+1]_z = ∑_{k<n} (k+1)·z^k`: the coefficient of\n`z^k` is `[k+1]_q` where the classical one is `k+1` (`gradedZInt_one`). Written with the\nbare geometric sum `∑_{j<k+1} q^j` rather than `qInt`, so the definition lives over a\n`CommRing`; see the predecessor files for the `[·]_q` naming over a `Field`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}